HIGH: Cross Function Guard Sibling: function 'approve' in contract 0xe219ec464ffabbe70d3bf192dc8c98aa5846b4f6 on BNB Chain [implementation behind proxy 0x8bd778b12b15416359a227f0533ce2d91844e1ed]

Cross-function reentrancy via an unguarded sibling: the contract DOES carry a reentrancy guard (nonReentrant / own lock) on some functions, but this sibling function is UNGUARDED, makes an external call and only writes state AFTER the call, while moving value or sharing state with a guarded function. A callee re-entering through this sibling during the guarded function's call (or vice versa) observes / mutates the shared state mid-flight - the guard gives a false sense of coverage. Observed: guarded sibling(s) ['mint', 'finishMinting'] exist, but 'approve' is unguarded, calls out and only then writes ['expirations']; it shares state ['address', 'uint256'] with the guarded function(s). Recipient analysis: recipient classified UNRESOLVED (no value destination found in function) - a stranger can receive value or control here, classified CANDIDATE under the receive rule.

Code:
function approve(address _to, uint256 _value) public returns (bool result) {
        result = super.approve(_to, _value);
        if (_value == uint256(-1)) {
            delete expirations[msg.sender][_to];
        }
    }

